    'Ἕωθεν προλέγειν ἑαυτῷ· συντεύξομαι περιέργῳ, ἀχαρίστῳ, ὑβριστῇ, δολερῷ, βασκάνῳ, ἀκοινωνήτῳ· πάντα ταῦτα συμβέβηκεν ἐκείνοις παρὰ τὴν ἄγνοιαν τῶν ἀγαθῶν καὶ κακῶν.',
    'Say to yourself at dawn: I shall encounter the meddlesome, the ungrateful, the arrogant, the treacherous, the envious, the uncharitable. All these things have befallen them through their ignorance of what is truly good and evil. But I, who have seen the nature of the good—that it is right, and the nature of evil—that it is wrong, and the nature of the wrongdoer himself—that he is akin to me, sharing not the same blood or seed but the same mind and portion of the divine—I cannot be harmed by any of them, for no one can involve me in what is degrading. Nor can I be angry with my kinsman or hate him.',
